WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.790
SPEAKER: Welcome to
this episode of Click

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.310
to Deploy, a series
where we show you

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.430
how easy it is to deploy
solutions from the Google Cloud

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.130
Marketplace.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.230
In this episode,
we'll be reviewing

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:15.080
how to set up a WordPress
website via the Cloud

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.490
Marketplace.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:18.590
In case you're
unfamiliar, WordPress

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.530
is a free open source
content management system

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:24.230
used to build custom websites.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:28.040
Let's start by searching for
WordPress in the Marketplace.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.148
Since WordPress is
open source, you'll

00:00:30.148 --> 00:00:31.940
see that we have a few
different deployment

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.190
options from different vendors.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.860
Each deployment may be deployed
with different operating

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:40.160
systems, security
patches, or server shapes.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.910
For this demo, we'll choose the
Google Click to Deploy Virtual

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:44.720
Machines option.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.640
The landing page will
display information

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.270
about the deployment,
pricing, and support.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:58.000
The pricing information
displays a high-level estimate

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.230
of charges.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.060
Once deployment
options are picked,

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:04.150
we'll receive a more
detailed breakdown.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.760
It's important to review
the additional details

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.290
to understand the technical
details for the deployment.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.040
We can see that a
single VM will be

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.620
deployed on Compute
Engine running the Debian

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.740
OS at version 9.13.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.780
We can also see all
of the packages that

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.700
will be installed on the VM.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.810
Let's begin the launch.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.160
Let's pick a deployment
name end zone.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.540
In this case, I'll go with
the default zone, US East 4b.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.310
I'll also stick with
the default machine

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:35.810
type, which is an E2 Small.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.050
The machine type chosen
should correspond

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.840
with how much traffic load you
are expecting on your WordPress

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.053
site.

00:01:47.053 --> 00:01:48.720
We'll choose an email
address that we'll

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.820
use for our PHP admin account.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.500
We'll also stick
with all the default

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.280
storage and networking options.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.520
Lastly, let's allow
source traffic

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.800
from the internet via
both HTTP and HTTPS.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.410
Before you deploy, you can
see your final estimate

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.980
of charges for the specific
options you choose to deploy.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.100
By clicking Details, you can
see how these costs break down.

00:02:20.965 --> 00:02:23.340
We'll launch and give our
deployment a minute to spin up.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.310
The next page will show
us all the information

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.560
about our specific deployment.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.260
Clicking the site address will
display our default WordPress

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:37.760
website.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.670
We're provided all of the
admin connectivity and login

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:48.690
information for both
WordPress and MySQL.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.180
We're also given the
ability to SSH directly

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.790
into our WordPress server.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.430
You'll also see that we're
given some suggested next steps,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.260
like changing
temporary passwords

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.050
and assigning a static
public IP address.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.645
Let's log in as the WordPress
admin and change the password.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.870
There we go.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.330
After we change MySQL
passwords, we're

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:24.550
ready to start customizing
our WordPress site

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.710
and configure a domain.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:30.350
If you enjoyed this video,
please subscribe and hit

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:31.850
the Like button below.

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:34.250
We encourage you to check
out the GCP marketplace

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.840
and deploy the
solution on your own.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.740
Thanks for watching.

